---
title: Label
description: Renders a label element bound to its Control's input, with for, id and error-state attributes wired automatically.
---

<script>
	import LiveFieldDemo from "$lib/demos/live-field-demo.svelte";
</script>

`Label` renders a `<label>` whose `for` attribute points at the input created by the enclosing [Control](/docs/components/control). You write the text; Formsnap resolves the association, the label's own `id`, and the `data-fs-*` styling hooks.

## Live example

Click the label text: focus moves to the input, because `Label` renders `for` pointing at the ID that `Control` generated.

<LiveFieldDemo />

## Minimal example

```svelte title="src/routes/settings/settings-form.svelte"
<script lang="ts">
	import { untrack } from "svelte";
	import { superForm, type SuperValidated } from "sveltekit-superforms";
	import { Control, Field, Label } from "@emmorts/formsnap";
	import type { SettingsData } from "./schema.js";

	let { validated }: { validated: SuperValidated<SettingsData> } = $props();

	const form = superForm(untrack(() => validated));
	const { form: formData } = form;
</script>

<Field {form} name="bio" fieldErrors>
	<Control>
		{#snippet children({ props })}
			<Label>Bio</Label>
			<textarea {...props} bind:value={$formData.bio}></textarea>
		{/snippet}
	</Control>
</Field>
```

## Replacing the rendered element

Use the `child` snippet to render your own element or component instead of a `<label>`. Spread the supplied `props` onto whatever you render — they carry the `for` association and the label registration.

```svelte title="src/routes/settings/styled-label.svelte"
<script lang="ts">
	import { Control, Label } from "@emmorts/formsnap";
	import { form, formData } from "./form.svelte.js";
</script>

<Control>
	{#snippet children({ props })}
		<Label>
			{#snippet child({ props: labelProps })}
				<span {...labelProps} class="field-label">Display name</span>
			{/snippet}
		</Label>
		<input {...props} bind:value={$formData.name} />
	{/snippet}
</Control>
```

## Props

| Prop | Type | Default | Description |
| ---- | ---- | ------- | ----------- |
| `id` | `string` | generated | The label's ID. Generated with Svelte's `$props.id()` when omitted, stable between server rendering and hydration. |
| `ref` | `HTMLLabelElement \| null` | `null` | Bindable reference to the rendered element. |
| `children` | `Snippet` | — | The label content. |
| `child` | `Snippet<[{ props }]`> | — | Replaces the `<label>` element entirely; spread the supplied `props` onto your own element. |
| `...rest` | `HTMLLabelAttributes` | — | Any other attribute (`class`, `style`, event handlers) is merged onto the label. |

The merged label attributes are `for` (the control's ID), `id`, `data-fs-label`, and `data-fs-error` when the field has errors. Attributes you pass win over nothing critical — `for` and the data attributes always come from Formsnap's side of the merge.

## Behavior notes

- **Requires a Control.** `Label` reads the enclosing [Control](/docs/components/control) from context; outside one it throws.
- **Naming participation.** The label registers itself with its control, and the most recently mounted label wins the control's headless naming ID (`useFormControl().labelProps.id`). A label rendered but not mounted does not contribute naming.
- **SSR-stable.** Because the label registers synchronously, the `for`/`id` pair is present in server-rendered HTML; no hydration flash in the association.
- **Error styling.** `data-fs-error` appears on the label while the field has errors, so you can tint the label without extra state.

## When to use the hooks instead

If your component library supplies its own label element, call `useFormControl` inside your component and spread `control.labelProps` onto it, or render your own visible label with `control.labelProps.id` as its ID. See [Custom controls](/docs/recipes/custom-controls).

## See also

- [Control](/docs/components/control) — the pairing partner that resolves the label's `for` target.
- [Description](/docs/components/description) — supplementary text, associated via `aria-describedby` rather than `for`.
- [Accessibility](/docs/guides/accessibility) — the full wiring model.
